NIRMUKA and the Desire to Outlive the Face

Introduction

We usually begin too late. We begin with the person already named,
already faced, already gathered into a biography. We ask whether this
person persists through time, whether memory, body, psychology,
narrative, or organismic life secures his identity. But a more difficult
question has already been avoided: why must the person first become
recognizable in order to count as a person at all? Why must there be a
face before there is an address, a name before there is accountability,
a biography before there is continuity? The familiar question of
personal identity asks how one remains the same through change. The
question pursued here is adjacent to, but not identical with, that
problem. It asks how the demand to remain recognizable can become one of
the ways in which a subject is bound to finitude.

I call this critical category nirmuka. The term is not offered as a
completed doctrine, nor as the name of a new metaphysical system. It
names a pressure within existing debates. nirmuka asks whether the forms
through which persons become socially and morally legible, face, name,
identity, and biography, may also become forms through which they are
exposed to classification, misrecognition, aging, narrative capture, and
eventual forgetting. This must not be overstated. I do not argue that
faces are inherently oppressive, that recognition is always domination,
or that identity is merely a prison. Such claims would be false, and
worse, careless. Faces can matter ethically. Recognition can be
necessary for dignity and political standing. Identity can sustain
memory, responsibility, and belonging. The claim is more restricted:
under certain conditions, the forms that make a person recognizable can
also require that person to remain available only in determinate,
repeatable, and socially legible ways.

The recent literature gives us the pieces of this problem, but not yet
the problem in this form. In contemporary philosophy of personal
identity, Madden (2024) begins with a useful warning. "Person," "self,"
and "personal identity" do not name one stable topic. They move between
metaphysical, legal, psychological, social, and representational uses.
Madden notes that Locke used "person" in relation to the public forum of
law and morality, while "self" named the reflective perspective from
which a being apprehends its own mind. But this neat distinction has not
survived. "Person" may now mean a publicly identifiable member of a
legal or moral community, while "self" may refer to oneself, an inner
aspect of oneself, a self-representation, a narrative self, a bodily
self, or a minimal self (Madden, 2024). Madden's clarification is
valuable because it resists the illusion that personal identity is one
simple object of inquiry. Yet the inquiry still tends to return to the
question "What are we?" or to the metaphysical question of what owns
experiences and persists through time. nirmuka does not reject that
question. It asks what the metaphysical inquiry often leaves
underexamined: how the public identifiability of the person can become a
demand that the subject remain readable as this one, under this name,
with this face, inside this biography.

A similar tension appears in recent work on recognition. Congdon and
Khurana (2026) rightly argue that recognition has moved far beyond a
narrow location in social and political philosophy. Their volume places
recognition across aesthetics, philosophy of mind and language, ethics,
social epistemology, political philosophy, race, gender, and alterity.
This expansion is important. It shows that recognition is not merely one
topic among others, but something closer to a transversal philosophical
category, one that informs how subjects appear, speak, know, suffer, and
are counted. Yet this expansion also sharpens a difficulty. If
recognition is so fundamental to the construction of a shared world,
then the danger of recognition is not only interpersonal insult or
failed respect. It may lie in the very configuration of what can appear
as a subject at all. Recognition gives entry, but entry often has terms.
nirmuka begins at the point where those terms become burdensome.

Gregson's recent discussion of Fanon makes this burden more precise.
Gregson (2025) refuses both the simple celebration and the simple
rejection of recognition. On his reading, Fanon sees misrecognition as a
real threat to freedom, especially because objectification can be
internalized, but he also warns that seeking self-worth through
recognition may give a false or self-undermining account of freedom.
Gregson calls this the "recognition trap": the mistake of treating
recognition as freedom itself. This is crucial for the present article
because it allows a restricted formulation. The problem is not
recognition as such. The problem is a regime in which a subject can be
recognized only by accepting the form in which recognition is prepared
to receive him. There is a difference between being seen and being fixed
by the condition of being seen. nirmuka names that difference, not as an
escape from social life, but as a critique of the point at which
recognizability becomes captivity.

Stahl (2024) gives another route into this structure. His account of
ideology as misrecognition argues that ideology does not always operate
by implanting false beliefs or by producing hostile interpersonal
attitudes. It can operate structurally, by limiting the capacity of
subjects to challenge dominant conceptual distinctions. The significance
of this argument for nirmuka is not that all identity is ideological.
That would be too quick. Rather, Stahl helps show how misrecognition may
occur through the very conceptual conditions under which agency becomes
intelligible. A subject may be constrained not only by what others
explicitly think about him, but by the forms of sense-making in which he
is allowed to appear as someone. The face and the name, in this sense,
are not only markers. They can become conceptual doors that open only by
narrowing what may pass through them.

The literature on the face brings the problem closer to its most visible
surface. Lambert's reading of Deleuze and Guattari treats faciality not
as a mere anatomical fact, but as an abstract machine that produces
collective or group faces. He is careful to note the political suspicion
surrounding any call to "dismantle the face," especially when such a
call is addressed to groups who have struggled to secure political
recognition in the first place. This caution is decisive. nirmuka cannot
be a romance of disappearance. It cannot command the vulnerable to
abandon the face when the face has barely been granted to them as a site
of dignity. But Lambert also shows why faciality remains philosophically
important: the face is not simply seen, it is organized, produced, and
made to function as a surface of social identification. The task, then,
is neither to worship the face nor to destroy it, but to ask under what
conditions the face becomes the form in which subjectivity is trapped.

Recent face studies confirm that this problem cannot be reduced to
Levinasian ethics alone. Levy and Leone's Masks and Faces treats masks
and faces as figures with ethical, political, scientific, visual, and
cultural force. The volume examines political personhood, racial
hierarchy, facial classification, prosthetic masks, surveillance,
deepfakes, and the unstable boundary between face and mask. Its value
for the present project is that it refuses the simple opposition between
an authentic face and a deceptive mask. Faces and masks are
co-constitutive surfaces through which identity, subjectivity, and
subjugation are constructed and contested. Yet this also reveals the gap
that nirmuka addresses. Face studies has shown that the face is
mediated, historical, technological, racialized, and political. It has
not yet sufficiently connected this mediation of the face to the desire
for personal immortality, that is, to the question of whether the wish
to outlive death may also be a wish to outlive the face as the mortal
surface of recognition.

The gap becomes sharper when immortality enters the discussion. Cave and
Fischer (2023) frame the question of living forever around desirability,
boredom, meaning, radical life extension, and the difference between
true immortality and extreme longevity. Their debate is not reducible to
fantasies of endless duration. It asks whether radically extended life
could remain humanly meaningful. But nirmuka asks a prior question: what
exactly must be preserved for a life to count as the continuation of
this person? If what is preserved is the recognizable profile of the
person, his face, name, memories, preferences, stories, and traits, then
immortality may remain tied to the same forms through which finitude
already finds him. This is not an argument against immortality. It is an
argument against assuming too quickly that personal immortality must
mean the endless preservation of the recognizable self.

The same assumption appears, in a different register, in recent debates
on digital replicas. Karpus and Strasser (2025) ask whether a digital
replica can be understood as an extension of someone's personhood by
drawing on Parfitian psychological connectedness and continuity. Their
account is careful because it does not simply equate replica and person;
it asks whether psychological data, preferences, memories, worldviews,
and traits might ground some weaker extension of personhood. But from
the standpoint of nirmuka, such digital afterlife projects reveal a
crucial ambiguity. They may promise survival beyond biological death
while intensifying dependence on the very archive of recognizability
that binds a person to biography. What survives is not the person
released from the face, but a legible profile constructed from traces
already captured.

This article therefore develops nirmuka as a critical category at the
intersection of personal identity, recognition, faciality, and
immortality. Its thesis is limited but unsettling: the desire for
personal immortality need not always be understood as the desire to
preserve the same recognizable self. Under certain conditions, it may
appear as a desire to be released from the face, name, identity, and
biography through which one is made available to finitude. nirmuka does
not deny the ethical importance of the face, the political necessity of
recognition, or the practical role of identity. It asks how these forms,
precisely because they are necessary, may also become dangerous when
they require the subject to remain forever findable under the same
mortal signs.

Etymology and Terminological Construction

The word nirmuka must be approached with caution. It would be tempting,
and perhaps too convenient, to present it as though it were already an
inherited Sanskrit concept waiting to be recovered. That temptation
should be resisted. The present argument does not require the claim that
nirmuka is an established technical term in classical Sanskrit
philosophy. It requires only a more modest claim: that nirmuka can be
responsibly constructed as a philosophical neologism from recognizable
Sanskritic elements, namely nir- or nis- and mukha. The term is
therefore not an appeal to ancient authority. It is a conceptual
construction. Its force comes not from pretending that the tradition has
already said what this article wishes to say, but from showing that the
semantic field of the term allows a philosophical problem to be named
with some precision.

This caution is especially important because Sanskrit lexicography
itself does not permit careless invention. Macdonell describes his
Sanskrit-English Dictionary as a work with "transliteration,
accentuation, and etymological analysis throughout," and in the preface
he explicitly distinguishes his work from lexicons that are not
etymological in the same sense. He also stresses that derivation matters
because it increases the linguistic and practical value of a word's
meaning (Macdonell, 1893). Apte, in a different but related spirit,
explains that his dictionary includes etymology for important words,
except where such etymology would be "purely fanciful." That
qualification is decisive for the present article. nirmuka should not be
protected by a fanciful etymology. Its philosophical use must remain
accountable to the lexical material from which it is formed (Apte,
1890).

The first element, nir-, is not merely a flat negation. Macdonell gives
nir- as equivalent to nis- before soft letters, and the entries that
follow show the prefix functioning privatively and separatively. Thus
nir-akṣara means illiterate, nir-agni means having no domestic fire,
nir-aṅkuśa means unfettered, unrestricted, perfectly free, and nir-aṅga
means incomplete or devoid of expedients. The pattern is not reducible
to a single English "not." It includes absence, deprivation, release,
lack, and freedom from a condition. The privative force of nir- can
therefore name more than an empty negation. It may name a relation of
departure from, deprivation of, or emancipation from what follows it
(Macdonell, 1893).

This matters for nirmuka. If the prefix were read only as "not," the
term would collapse into a simple adjective: faceless, without a face,
lacking a face. But if nir- is allowed its wider force, the term becomes
more philosophically charged. It can name a movement away from the face,
a release from the face, or a freedom from the conditions imposed by the
face. The difference is not cosmetic. A merely faceless being remains
defined by the absence of what recognition expects to find. It is still
governed by the face, though negatively. nirmuka, as I use it, is not
simply the condition of having no face. It is the critical movement by
which the subject is no longer exhausted by the forms of recognizability
that the face organizes.

The second element, mukha, is even more important. Macdonell's entry
does not restrict mukha to the anatomical face. It means mouth, jaws,
face, snout, muzzle, and beak, but also direction, quarter, spout, mouth
or opening, entrance, forepart, tip, point, edge, surface, top, upper
side, head, chief, beginning, source, occasion, and means (Macdonell,
1893). This range is crucial. Mukha is not only the face as visible
countenance. It is also the mouth as opening, the entrance through which
something becomes accessible, the surface through which something
presents itself, the front by which something is approached, and the
beginning or source from which something proceeds. The semantic field of
mukha is therefore not merely anatomical. It is topological, semiotic,
and relational.

Here the word begins to tremble. If mukha meant only "face," nirmuka
would remain close to "facelessness." But if mukha also means surface,
opening, entrance, front, beginning, source, and means, then nirmuka can
name a departure from the entire apparatus by which a subject is made
available to recognition. The face is not simply one bodily part among
others. It is the front at which the person is expected to appear. It is
the opening through which speech, expression, and address are expected
to pass. It is the surface upon which signs of age, emotion, race,
gender, health, grief, fatigue, and supposed interiority are read. It is
the beginning of identification, the first place where others often try
to say: here is the person.

But must the person begin there? Must the face be the first guarantee of
presence? Must the name come next, then biography, then the whole
archive of identity? nirmuka does not answer these questions by
abolishing the face. It does not say that the face is false, or that the
person must escape every possible form of visibility. That would again
overstate the matter. Rather, nirmuka gives a name to the suspicion that
the face, precisely because it enables recognition, can also become the
surface through which the subject is captured. The face may welcome
ethical address. It may also become the place where the subject is fixed
too quickly.

This terminological construction also prevents a common
misunderstanding. nirmuka is not identical with anonymity. Anonymity is
usually the absence or concealment of a name within a system that still
understands naming as the norm. The anonymous remains legible as the one
whose name is missing. Nor is nirmuka identical with self-erasure.
Self-erasure would imply the disappearance or destruction of the
subject. nirmuka names something more restricted: a withdrawal from the
demand that the subject be exhausted by face, name, biography, and
stable identity. It is not the refusal to appear at all. It is the
refusal to let appearance become captivity.

Nor should nirmuka be confused with the mere horror of the featureless.
The featureless face often terrifies because it interrupts the ordinary
grammar of recognition. But nirmuka is not a horror-image. It does not
celebrate the blank head or the missing visage. Such images remain
trapped within a visual economy of lack. They frighten because
recognition reaches for the face and finds an absence. The philosophical
point is different. nirmuka asks whether the desire to become more than
one's mortal form may involve a release from the very structures that
make the subject recognizable as a finite person.

The term, then, can be defined provisionally as follows: nirmuka names a
critical movement away from mukha, away from the face, surface, opening,
front, and recognizable form through which a subject is made readable.
Its target is not the face as such, but the regime in which the face
becomes the privileged entrance to personhood. Its concern is not mere
disappearance, but the possibility that a subject may not be reducible
to the signs by which he is found. This is why nirmuka is inseparable
from the problem of personal immortality. If what is preserved forever
is only the recognizable face, the stable name, the narrated biography,
and the identity already available to others, then immortality may
preserve the very forms through which finitude holds the person. nirmuka
asks whether the desire to outlive death may also be, under certain
conditions, a desire to outlive the face.

The Face as Exposure and Capture

The argument cannot move from mukha to nirmuka without passing through
the face. But here, too, one must move slowly. The face is not an
innocent object. Nor is it simply a surface to be condemned. It stands
at the threshold of the person, but thresholds do not only open. They
also regulate entry. They tell us where one may begin, what must be
shown, what must be withheld, and what will count as an appearance. The
face exposes the subject, but exposure is not yet freedom. To be exposed
is to be available, vulnerable, addressable, visible. It is also to be
read.

This ambiguity is partly obscured when the face is treated only as a
site of ethical revelation. A broadly Levinasian account gives the face
an extraordinary dignity: the face is not merely seen, not merely
processed as a visual object, not merely inserted into a taxonomy. It
interrupts. It calls. It places the subject under responsibility. But if
the face is approached only in this way, one danger appears at once: the
face becomes too pure, too quickly redeemed, too easily protected from
its other histories. It becomes the place where ethics arrives, while
the violence of facial legibility is displaced elsewhere. nirmuka does
not require us to reject the ethical force of the face. It asks whether
the face, precisely because it can summon responsibility, can also
become a privileged surface of capture.

Sealey's reading of Levinas through Blanchot is useful because it
prevents a sentimental reading of the face. Sealey argues that Levinas's
account of the face cannot be separated from the il y a, the anonymous
and impersonal field of existence. On her reading, the ethical import of
the face lies in its being "without determination or nomenclature," but
this does not make the encounter peaceful. It also means acknowledging
"the underlying horror" of the ethical encounter. The face does not
simply give a person to us in a stable and reassuring form. It is bound
to alterity, impersonality, ambiguity, and a kind of disaster in which
the subject is displaced from mastery (Sealey, 2013).

This is decisive for nirmuka. If the face, in its ethical force, is
"without determination or nomenclature," then the face cannot be reduced
to the socially recognizable face. It cannot be identical with the
passport face, the family face, the racialized face, the professional
face, the gendered face, the face others claim to know. The Levinasian
face, at its strongest, does not confirm a subject's place within
recognition. It troubles that place. It resists being put to work. It is
not the sign by which I safely know who the other is. It is the
interruption of that very knowledge.

But this raises a difficulty. If the ethical face must remain without
determination, then what happens when social life demands determination?
What happens when the face is no longer allowed to remain abstract,
ambiguous, or excessive, but must be assigned a name, a category, a
file, a story, a risk profile, a role? The face that resists
determination becomes, in practice, a face demanded by systems of
determination. Here the ambiguity turns. The same surface that may
interrupt knowledge may also be forced to serve knowledge. It may be
converted into evidence. It may be made to say too much.

This is why the face cannot be understood only phenomenologically. It
must also be understood semiotically and politically. Lambert's reading
of Deleuze and Guattari is crucial here. He does not treat the face as a
natural organ that simply expresses inner life. He reads faciality as an
abstract machine that produces collective or group faces. The face is
not merely the bearer of expression; it becomes a dispositif of
political subjectivity. Lambert's examples are deliberately
uncomfortable: white face, black face, brown face, girl face, boy face.
The point is not that these faces are imaginary. The point is that
social and political orders may require subjects to appear through such
faces, and may then confuse that requirement with liberation itself
(Lambert, 2023).

The everyday scene already shows this machinery at work. In
conversation, as Lambert notes, the face functions as a dense semiotic
surface: the listener uses the face of the speaker to stabilize what
words might mean. Is she angry? Is he bored? Did the statement mean
refusal, hesitation, contempt, desire? The face guides interpretation
before interpretation admits that it needed guidance. What appears to be
immediate expression is already caught within a semiotic economy. The
face does not merely accompany speech. It helps decide what speech will
be taken to have meant. In this sense, the face is not secondary to
language. It participates in the production of sense.

There is nothing necessarily sinister in this. Human beings need
gestures, tones, glances, and expressions. A world without such surfaces
would not be a world of pure freedom. It might be a world of terrifying
opacity. But the danger lies in the passage from interpretive support to
interpretive capture. The face helps others understand us, but it also
tempts them to finish us. It offers signs, and signs invite premature
closure. A tired face becomes guilt. A silent face becomes arrogance. A
marked face becomes destiny. A young face becomes innocence or
incompetence. An old face becomes decline. The face is read before the
subject has spoken enough to contest the reading.

This is not merely a problem of individual prejudice. It is also
historical. Levy and Leone's Masks and Faces shows that faces and masks
carry ethical and political meanings within scientific, cultural,
visual, and philosophical traditions. The volume examines how faces and
masks have shaped political personhood, racial hierarchy, facial
classification, surveillance, and the boundary between the human and
non-human. This matters because it dislodges the belief that the face is
simply given by nature. The face is not only encountered. It is also
trained, classified, compared, restored, beautified, racialized,
anonymized, and technologized (Levy & Leone, 2026).

The introduction to Masks and Faces is especially useful because it
refuses the familiar opposition between the authentic face and the
deceptive mask. We often say that faces reveal and masks conceal. But
Levy and Leone show that this opposition is too simple. Faces themselves
may function like masks in social life, while masks may enable rather
than merely obstruct appearance. They cite Goffman's notion of "face
work" to show that the face is not simply lodged in or on the body, but
is associated with social value claimed in interaction. A face is
managed. It is adjusted to context and audience. It is not merely an
organic surface. It is a social instrument of presentation and
regulation.

This point must be handled carefully. It would be wrong to say that the
face is "nothing but" a mask. Such a claim would erase embodiment and
vulnerability too quickly. But it is equally wrong to say that the face
is simply the natural truth beneath all masks. The face is already
mediated. It is already exposed to norms. It is already drawn into
practices of interpretation, correction, and recognition. The face does
not wait outside history. It enters history at the very point where
others begin to say what it means.

The case of prosthetic masks for disfigured soldiers, discussed in Masks
and Faces, makes this ambiguity more concrete. These masks were not
merely disguises. They functioned as reinventions of the face,
boundaries that enclosed and reconstructed the subject. The mask could
conceal injury, but it could also restore a face to social appearance.
The wounded face was not only a medical problem; it was a problem of
recognizability, dignity, and social return. Family members might recoil
from the wounded face, not because the person had ceased to exist, but
because the ordinary grammar of facial recognition had been broken. The
mask-made face thus exposes a cruel dependency: one may need a face in
order to be received again as human.

Here nirmuka must refuse an easy conclusion. It cannot say, "therefore,
abandon the face." For the wounded soldier, the restored face may be the
condition of returning to the world. For racialized, colonized,
gendered, and politically erased subjects, the struggle to have a face
may be inseparable from the struggle to be counted. Lambert notes this
difficulty in relation to feminist suspicion toward Deleuze and
Guattari's call to dismantle the face: if one has only just begun to
secure a politically recognizable face, then the command to lose the
face may sound like another demand for disappearance.

This objection is not external to nirmuka. It belongs at its center.
nirmuka is not a universal command to become faceless. It is not a
politics that asks vulnerable subjects to surrender the fragile forms of
recognition they have fought to obtain. Its claim is narrower. The face
becomes dangerous when it ceases to be a site of appearance and becomes
the condition under which appearance is policed. It becomes capture when
the subject is allowed to appear only as this face, with this meaning,
within this category, under this history.

The face, then, is double. It exposes and captures. It opens the subject
to address, but it also makes the subject available to social fixation.
It may interrupt my freedom by calling me to responsibility, but it may
also interrupt the other's freedom by making the other legible too
quickly. The question is not whether we should affirm or reject the
face. That opposition is too blunt. The more difficult question is this:
when does the face remain an opening, and when does it become a trap?

nirmuka begins from this unstable point. It does not negate the face. It
contests the sovereignty of the face as the privileged entrance to
personhood. It asks whether a subject can be more than the surface
through which he is recognized, more than the signs gathered on his
face, more than the biography others attach to that face. But this
question immediately leads to another. If the face is the surface
through which the subject is received, then recognition is the social
act that confirms or refuses that reception. The next problem,
therefore, is not the face alone, but recognition: the moment when
exposure becomes acknowledgment, or when acknowledgment becomes capture.

Recognition and the Trap of Identity

If the face is the surface through which the subject is received,
recognition is the act through which that reception is confirmed,
denied, or regulated. But recognition is never a simple light falling
upon a subject who was already there in full clarity. It is not only the
affirmation of what exists. It also helps determine what can appear as
worthy of affirmation. This is why the problem of the face must pass
into the problem of recognition. A face may expose the subject, but
recognition decides whether this exposure will be received as dignity,
threat, abnormality, dependency, competence, humanity, or nothing at
all. The subject comes before others, but not before a neutral world. He
comes before forms of intelligibility already armed with names.

Recognition theory is often attractive because it seems to repair the
loneliness of the subject. Against the fantasy of an isolated self, it
insists that freedom is relational. Visser's reconstruction of the
Hegelian tradition states the basic point clearly: recognition denotes a
social and relational form of freedom, since a subject is free only
insofar as another recognizes that subject's freedom, and vice versa
(Visser, 2024). This is not a minor correction to liberal individualism.
It changes the grammar of personhood. The subject does not first possess
freedom privately and then ask society to respect it. Freedom itself
becomes dependent on social mediation. One cannot be free alone. Yet
this insight contains its own danger. If freedom depends on recognition,
then the subject may become dependent on the very forms through which
recognition is granted. The door opens, but the door has a frame.

This is why the optimistic account of recognition is insufficient on its
own. Visser distinguishes a more optimistic line, running from Hegel
through Mead, Habermas, Taylor, and Honneth, from a more ambivalent or
pessimistic line, running from Hegel through Kojève, Althusser,
Foucault, and Butler. The first tends to treat recognition as a
condition of freedom whose failures can, in principle, be remedied. The
second treats recognition as both enabling and constraining freedom at
the same time (Visser, 2024). nirmuka belongs closer to the second line,
but it cannot simply adopt its most radical gestures. Recognition cannot
be dismissed as domination. For many subjects, the absence of
recognition is not liberation but social death, humiliation,
invisibility, exposure to violence, or exclusion from rights. The point
is narrower: precisely because recognition may be necessary, it may also
become dangerous. It can become the form in which the subject is rescued
only by being fixed.

The recent expansion of recognition studies makes this danger more
visible. Congdon and Khurana argue that recognition is no longer
confined to social and political philosophy. It now crosses aesthetics,
philosophy of mind and language, social epistemology, ethics, race,
gender, and political theory. They even suggest that recognition begins
to resemble categories such as normativity, concept, or subjectivity,
categories that traverse philosophical fields rather than belonging to
one subdiscipline (Congdon & Khurana, 2026). This expansion is valuable,
but it also generates a problem. If recognition is everywhere, it risks
losing critical sharpness. A concept that explains aesthetic response,
moral address, epistemic agency, social status, political inclusion, and
ethical relation may become too generous. It may name the entire field
of human intelligibility without telling us where recognition becomes
coercive. nirmuka sharpens the issue by asking not only whether a
subject is recognized, but at what cost recognition becomes available.

The cost is clearest when recognition presupposes what it claims to
grant. Some forms of recognition do not open a new space for the
subject. They confirm the subject only under a description already
available within a social world. The subject is recognized as worker,
citizen, patient, woman, migrant, believer, criminal, victim, genius,
failure, child, elder, foreigner, body, race. These terms may be
necessary. Some may be politically indispensable. But each can become a
small prison when it no longer functions as a partial description and
begins to function as the condition of intelligibility itself.
Recognition then says: you may appear, but only here; you may speak, but
only as this; you may be known, but only by returning to the form in
which we already know how to receive you.

Gregson's reading of Fanon gives the clearest recent formulation of this
problem. Fanon's relation to recognition, on Gregson's account, is
neither simple endorsement nor simple rejection. Misrecognition is
genuinely damaging. In the scene of racial objectification, Fanon does
not merely encounter an external insult; he confronts a world that
returns his body to him through histories, stereotypes, and inherited
narratives. Gregson stresses that Fanon takes seriously the intrinsic
harms of misrecognition, including the way objectification can be
internalized. But Gregson also shows that Fanon refuses to make
recognition itself the final name of freedom. The "recognition trap"
consists in mistaking recognition for freedom itself (Gregson, 2025).
This formulation is crucial. Recognition may be necessary for freedom
without being identical to freedom. It may be a condition, a mediation,
a partial achievement, but not the thing itself.

The trap is subtle because recognition often arrives as relief. The
subject who has been misnamed, degraded, or excluded seeks a
counter-recognition. He wants to be seen otherwise. This desire is not
foolish. It may be morally and politically necessary. But the trap
begins when the subject seeks the source of self-worth from the very
order that made his humanity questionable. The master recognizes, but he
recognizes from the position of mastery. The dominant society includes,
but it includes by translating the subject into forms it can manage. The
colonized subject is seen, but only after being asked to become visible
according to the grammar of the colonizer. This is why nirmuka cannot be
reduced to a rejection of recognition. Its question is more exacting:
when does the struggle to be recognized become a struggle to be admitted
into the very scene that must be overcome?

Gregson avoids the opposite error as well. Fanon, on his reading, does
not advise abandoning recognitive reciprocity. The article ends by
emphasizing that Fanon's insights cut across struggles for recognition,
freedom, and belonging, and that recognition can play both
freedom-affirming and freedom-denying roles. Fanon's alternative is not
pure withdrawal, but freedom as an open-ended process of
self-constitution in which certain forms of recognition serve a
necessary but subordinate function (Gregson, 2025). This is exactly the
kind of qualification nirmuka needs. It is not a doctrine of
disappearance. It does not say that freedom lies outside every
recognitive relation. It says that recognition must remain subordinate
to the possibility of becoming otherwise. When recognition ceases to
serve self-constitution and instead demands self-repetition, identity
hardens into bondage.

Stahl's account of ideology as misrecognition extends the point beyond
the interpersonal scene. Ideology, he argues, should not be understood
only as false belief. It may operate by constraining epistemic agency,
by limiting the capacity of subjects to challenge dominant conceptual
distinctions. On his expressivist account, ideological discourses
reflect the rules of hierarchical social practices and impose limits on
how subjects can make sense of the world and their needs. Such
misrecognition need not involve hostile attitudes on the part of
particular persons. It can be structural rather than interpersonal
(Stahl, 2024). This matters for nirmuka because the bondage of identity
is not always produced by someone saying, "I refuse you." It may be
produced by a social grammar in which only certain forms of
self-description are available. The subject may be misrecognized even
when no one means to injure him, because the available categories
already decide too much.

Stahl's analysis also helps avoid a shallow critique of recognition. If
misrecognition is structural, then the answer cannot be merely better
attitudes. One cannot simply ask individuals to look more generously at
the face before them. The problem may lie in the conceptual distinctions
through which the face is interpreted. A subject can be recognized
kindly and still be captured. He can be affirmed as resilient,
authentic, productive, vulnerable, diverse, employable, exceptional, or
inspiring, and each affirmation may still tighten the form in which he
is allowed to appear. The kindness of recognition does not guarantee its
freedom. Sometimes recognition flatters the cage.

Butler's relevance lies here. In Visser's reconstruction, Butler treats
recognition as mediated and regulated by social norms that exceed the
dyad of recognizer and recognized. The encounter between subjects is
already framed by a language and by norms concerning what will and will
not constitute recognizability. Recognition, subjection, and
subjectivation can coincide. One becomes a subject by being hailed, but
also becomes subjected through that very hail (Visser, 2024). This is
one of the most important lessons for nirmuka: the subject who turns
toward recognition may be born into social intelligibility at the very
moment he is bound by it. The call "you there" does not merely identify
him. It positions him. It gives him a place from which he can answer,
but it also defines the terms of the answer.

In contemporary organizational life, Visser shows how recognition can
become instrumental. Workers may be recognized as "employees of the
month," but only insofar as they achieve narrowly defined performance
goals. Female workers may be valued for care and support tasks in ways
that reinforce gender stereotypes. Recognition here does not fail by
being absent. It fails by being too conditional, too useful to the
institution that grants it. The person is recognized, but only through
traits that keep the system intact. This provides a concrete model for
nirmuka. Identity becomes a trap not only when it is denied, but when it
is rewarded under conditions that reproduce the subject's containment.

This section's claim can now be stated more directly. Identity becomes
bondage when recognition no longer allows the subject to appear, but
requires the subject to remain readable in a determinate form. This does
not mean that all identity is bondage. It does not mean that recognition
should be abandoned. It means that recognition contains a recurrent
danger: the danger that the subject will be granted social existence
only through the repetition of a legible identity. The face is
recognized as the same face. The name is returned as the same name. The
biography is retold as the same story. What was supposed to rescue the
subject from invisibility becomes the mechanism by which he is made
permanently findable.

nirmuka emerges at the point where recognition begins to tremble. It
asks whether the subject can be more than the identity through which he
is recognized. It asks whether freedom requires not the destruction of
recognition, but the refusal to let recognition exhaust the subject's
possible being. And this leads directly to the next problem. If
recognition stabilizes the subject through face, name, and biography,
then personal identity appears not merely as a metaphysical question of
persistence through time, but as a demand to remain the same for others.
The question is no longer only "What makes me me?" It is also: who
requires me to remain recognizable as myself?

Nirmuka as a Critical Category

The preceding sections have brought us to the point where nirmuka can be
stated positively. Not fully, not finally, and not as though a single
concept could solve the old quarrels concerning face, recognition,
identity, and death. A concept that claims too much becomes useless
almost at once. It shines for a moment, then hardens into rhetoric.
nirmuka should not be allowed that comfort. It is not a doctrine of
facelessness. It is not the thesis that identity must be destroyed. It
is not the claim that recognition is always a trap, or that the face is
always a prison. Its claim is narrower, and for that reason more
difficult: under certain conditions, the forms through which a person
becomes recognizable may also become the forms through which that person
is bound to finitude.

The word itself already warns us against simplification. From the
earlier etymological construction, nir- does not merely mean "not." It
may indicate absence, deprivation, release, separation, or freedom from
a condition. Mukha, likewise, does not mean only the anatomical face. It
also names the mouth, opening, entrance, front, surface, beginning,
source, occasion, and means. The word nirmuka, then, should not be
reduced to the flat image of a faceless head. It names a movement away
from mukha: away from the face as surface, entrance, front, and
privileged form of recognizability. This movement is not necessarily a
disappearance. It is a refusal to let the subject be exhausted by the
opening through which others claim to find him.

This distinction is essential. Facelessness remains too dependent on the
face. It names the failure of the face to appear, and therefore still
belongs to the expectation that the face should have appeared. The
faceless subject remains a scandal inside the regime of faciality. He is
still measured by the missing sign. nirmuka is different. It does not
ask us to imagine a subject without features, without embodiment,
without visibility. It asks whether personhood must be governed by the
demand to present itself through a face, a name, a biography, and a
stable identity. Its question is not, "Can there be a person with no
face?" Its question is sharper: "Why has the face been allowed to become
the entrance through which the person must pass?"

As a critical category, nirmuka has three interlocking claims. The first
is the privative claim. The privative movement of nirmuka is not mere
negation. It does not say: no face, no name, no identity, no
recognition. Such negations are theatrical but philosophically poor. The
privative force of nirmuka concerns release from capture. A subject may
have a face and yet not be reducible to it. A subject may bear a name
and yet not be exhausted by the biography attached to that name. A
subject may require recognition and yet resist the demand that
recognition fix him in one legible form. nirmuka therefore names a
negative movement only in a restricted sense: it negates the sovereignty
of recognizability, not the possibility of appearing.

The second claim is the facial claim. The face is not only an empirical
surface. Nor is it only an ethical epiphany. Recent work on faciality
shows that faces are produced, coded, circulated, and made to bear
social and political meanings. In the discussion of faciality in The
Hybrid Face, Deleuze and Guattari are read as treating the face not as a
universal human token, but as an abstract machine that constructs a type
of human through overcoding. The face becomes a surface where
vulnerability is converted into type, where coherent facial units and
deviations are organized through historically specific forms of power.
The face, in this sense, is not simply what appears before recognition.
It is already shaped by the regimes that make recognition possible.

This is why nirmuka cannot remain at the level of Levinasian reverence
for the face, even though it need not reject Levinas. The face may call
me to responsibility; but under other conditions, the face may be made
to serve classification. It may become a document before it becomes an
appeal. It may be read as race, gender, age, danger, innocence,
exhaustion, inferiority, or competence before the subject has spoken
enough to contest that reading. Masks and Faces makes this point
especially clear when it discusses faciality as a politics that computes
margins of normality and racializes the entire body through a faciality
machine. The face is denaturalized: one does not simply "have" a face,
but may slide into one through historically contingent assemblages of
power.

The third claim is the recognitional claim. Recognition is necessary,
but it is not innocent. Gregson's reading of Fanon is crucial because it
refuses both the naïve celebration of recognition and the equally naïve
fantasy that freedom can simply do without it. Misrecognition harms the
subject, including through objectification and internalization; yet
recognition can also become a trap when it is mistaken for freedom
itself. For nirmuka, this is not merely a political problem. It is also
a problem of personal form. A person may become socially real only by
accepting the form in which a recognitive order is prepared to receive
him. In that case, recognition does not merely see the subject. It
installs the subject inside a recognizable identity and then asks him to
live there.

Here nirmuka turns the problem of identity slightly away from its usual
path. The traditional question asks what makes a person the same through
time. Is it memory, psychological continuity, bodily persistence,
narrative unity, animal life, or some other relation? That question
remains important. But nirmuka asks another question: what happens when
the need to remain the same becomes a demand imposed by others? The
subject is not only metaphysically continuous. He is socially returned.
Returned to the same face. Returned to the same name. Returned to the
same story. Returned, again and again, to the signs by which others know
where to find him. The violence here is not always spectacular. It may
be gentle, administrative, familial, affectionate, therapeutic,
academic. "We know you," people say. But perhaps that is sometimes the
beginning of the danger.

This danger should not be exaggerated. Identity is not only captivity.
It can shelter the subject from erasure. It can make responsibility
possible. It can preserve memory, sustain legal protection, and support
political struggle. For subjects denied public standing, the demand to
be recognized under a name and face may be urgent. This is why nirmuka
must not become an irresponsible romance of anonymity. It would be cruel
to tell the unrecognized to abandon recognition, or to tell the
disfigured, racialized, colonized, or politically erased that the face
does not matter. Sometimes the face must be claimed before it can be
questioned. Sometimes the name must be defended before it can be
loosened.

But the reverse danger is also real. Because identity is often
necessary, it is easily sanctified. Because recognition can be
liberating, it is easily mistaken for liberation itself. Gregson's
Fanonian formulation helps retain the tension: recognition may be
essential but partial, freedom-affirming in some contexts and
freedom-denying in others. nirmuka belongs to that tension. It does not
abolish recognition; it subordinates recognition to the possibility that
the subject may exceed every form in which he is recognized.

The fourth claim, which gathers the others, is the immortality claim.
nirmuka is not only a theory of face or recognition. It is a way of
rethinking the desire for personal immortality. Usually, to want
immortality is to want to continue as oneself. But what is this
"oneself" that must continue? If it means only the perpetuation of a
recognizable profile, the same face, same name, same memories, same
preferences, same biography, then immortality may preserve the very
structure through which finitude already possesses the subject. It may
extend life without releasing the person from the mortal signs by which
he is located.

This is why recent debates on digital replicas are philosophically
useful, even though nirmuka is not a theory of digital afterlife. Karpus
and Strasser ask whether digital replicas can count as extensions of
personhood through psychological connectedness. They are careful not to
identify replicas straightforwardly with persons; they discuss "slivers"
of personhood, graded connectedness, and grey areas where the question
of continuation may not have a categorical answer. From the standpoint
of nirmuka, this carefulness is valuable, but the deeper assumption
remains revealing. Digital immortality often imagines survival as the
persistence of recognizable traits. A person continues if enough of his
psychological, stylistic, or biographical pattern can be reconstructed.
But this is precisely what nirmuka questions: must survival mean the
preservation of recognizability?

The digital replica makes the question almost unbearable. It promises
survival by archive: messages, images, preferences, voice, memory,
style. Yet the archive is also a cage. What survives is what has already
been captured. The subject is extended through traces, but the traces
belong to the order of legibility. The replica may speak in one's style,
but style is not the living excess of the person. It may preserve one's
sayings, but sayings are already detached. It may continue a pattern,
but a pattern is precisely what can be repeated without the one who once
interrupted it. nirmuka does not deny that such continuities can matter.
It denies only that they exhaust the desire to outlive death.

The central definition can now be stated. nirmuka is a critical category
for thinking the desire for personal immortality as a possible release
from the face, name, identity, biography, and forms of recognition that
bind the subject to finitude. Each word in this definition matters. It
is a "critical category," not a metaphysical proof. It concerns a
"possible release," not a universal obligation. It speaks of forms that
"bind," not forms that always enslave. It links immortality to face and
recognition, not because all immortality must pass through them, but
because much contemporary thinking about personal survival assumes that
what must survive is a recognizable person.

This definition also clarifies what nirmuka is not. It is not nihilism,
because it does not say that the person is nothing. It is not
self-erasure, because it does not seek the destruction of the subject.
It is not ordinary anonymity, because anonymity still belongs to the
regime of the name, defining the subject as the one whose name is hidden
or missing. It is not simply becoming-imperceptible, because it remains
concerned with the ethical and political importance of appearing. It is
not digital immortality, because it does not seek survival through the
preservation of one's data profile. It is not anti-recognition, because
it admits that recognition may be necessary for dignity, rights, and
belonging. It is a critique of recognizability when recognizability
becomes a condition of captivity.

One might say, then, that nirmuka begins with a refusal, but it does not
end there. It refuses the reduction of the person to the face. It
refuses the equation of recognition with freedom. It refuses the
assumption that personal identity is only a problem of persistence
rather than also a problem of social return. It refuses the idea that
immortality must mean indefinite preservation of the same recognizable
self. But after these refusals, something remains: not a faceless void,
not a pure anonymity, not a liberated essence untouched by history, but
a subject whose possible being exceeds the forms in which he has been
made available to others.

The task of nirmuka is therefore not to remove the person from the
world. It is to prevent the world from closing too quickly around the
person. It asks that the face remain an opening rather than a verdict,
that recognition remain a relation rather than a sentence, that identity
remain usable without becoming sovereign, and that immortality be
thinkable as more than the indefinite survival of one's socially
readable form. Perhaps this is all that can be claimed. But it is enough
to disturb the usual order of the questions. We ask how the person
persists. nirmuka asks whether the person must always be returned to the
signs by which persistence is recognized. We ask whether someone can
live forever. nirmuka asks whether living forever as the same
recognizable self would still be freedom, or whether it would be only
the prolongation of the face.

Conclusion

This article has developed nirmuka as a critical category for thinking
the relation between face, recognition, personal identity, and the
desire for immortality. Its claim has been deliberately restricted.
nirmuka does not deny that faces matter, that recognition can be
necessary, or that identity may sustain responsibility, memory, and
political standing. A subject without recognition may not be free. A
subject without a name may not be protected. A subject without a face
may not be received. These truths must not be dismissed too quickly. Yet
they do not settle the matter. They only make the danger more difficult
to see.

The danger appears when the forms that enable the subject to appear also
begin to determine in advance how the subject may appear. The face
becomes not only an opening, but a surface of interpretation. The name
becomes not only an address, but a point of return. Biography becomes
not only a story, but a closure. Identity becomes not only continuity,
but a demand. Recognition then no longer merely welcomes the subject. It
fixes the subject in a form that can be repeated, administered,
remembered, classified, and judged. The subject is saved from
invisibility, but only by being made findable under signs that may not
exhaust him.

nirmuka names this tension. It does not ask that the subject disappear.
It asks whether appearing must always mean appearing through the face,
the name, the stable biography, and the recognizable identity. It does
not reject recognition. It asks when recognition ceases to serve the
subject's becoming and begins to require self-repetition. It does not
reject personal identity. It asks whether the question of persistence
has been too quickly separated from the social demand to remain the
same. The usual problem asks what makes a person continue through time.
nirmuka asks who, or what, requires that continuation to remain
recognizable.

This is why nirmuka also modifies the question of immortality. The
desire to live forever is often understood as the desire to preserve
oneself indefinitely. But if the self to be preserved is already the
face-bound, name-bound, biography-bound, and recognitively fixed self,
then immortality may not overcome finitude as decisively as it promises.
It may prolong the forms through which finitude already holds the
subject. To live forever as the same recognizable self may be one way of
surviving death. It may also be one way of extending the captivity of
identity.

The conclusion, then, is not that immortality is impossible,
undesirable, or incoherent. Nor is it that the face, recognition, and
identity must be abandoned. The conclusion is more limited: any account
of personal immortality that assumes the preservation of recognizability
must be questioned. What survives when the face survives? What is
preserved when the name is preserved? What remains when biography is
extended beyond death?

nirmuka does not answer these questions by offering a final metaphysics
of survival. It leaves them open, but not untouched. It asks us to think
of immortality not only as persistence, but also as possible release
from the mortal signs by which a person is made knowable. To outlive
death, perhaps, is not always to remain forever recognizable. Sometimes
the more difficult desire is to outlive the face.

